<UserControl x:Class="Bws.Gui.DetailsView"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             Focusable="False"
             IsTabStop="False"
             Grid.IsSharedSizeScope="True"
             Width="{StaticResource WidthDetailsPanel}">

    <!--
        EVERYTHING THIS WINDOW KNOWS ABOUT ONE ENTRY - the second half of `S7`, built 2026-08-13,
        and DRAWN AGAIN ON 2026-09-16 after the owner said it was black text on grey that could not
        be seen. He was right to the pixel: tools/gui-probe/details-shot.ps1 found the values drawn
        in #000000 on #202020, a ratio of 1.29, because their only style was declared here, based on
        the implicit TextBlock style, and named no colour. What replaced it is
        docs/PROJEKT-SZCZEGOLY-20260916.md, and this file is that design and nothing more: every
        face below is a name in Themes/Details.xaml, where a guard reads it.

        A UserControl RATHER THAN A TEMPLATE IN THE THEME, AND THAT IS A DECISION ABOUT WHAT KIND OF
        THING THIS IS. The theme files hold the LOOK OF A CONTROL - a chip, a cell, a heading. This is
        a PART OF THE WINDOW, like the list and the row of filters, and a part of the window with its
        own file is what a UserControl is for. It also gets what a template cannot: a code-behind
        class, so the close button needs no lookup by name.

        THE WIDTH IS A NAME NOW. It was a number written here from 2026-08-13, with a paragraph
        saying that Values.xaml stood on the markup ceiling and had no room for one - the one
        deviation from `ADR-23` this panel confessed to. That file has room today, so the number
        lives there under WidthDetailsPanel, with the reason it is fixed rather than shared.

        Focusable and IsTabStop are off because a ContentControl - which is what a UserControl is -
        is BOTH BY DEFAULT, measured rather than assumed on 2026-08-13. Without them the panel is a
        tab stop that does nothing, in front of the close button that does. `docs/10` trap 8.
    -->

    <UserControl.Style>
        <Style TargetType="UserControl">
            <!--
                CLOSED UNTIL SOMEBODY ASKS, and it collapses rather than hiding so the column it
                sits in gives its width back to the list. The layout file of `S6d3` deliberately
                does not remember whether it was open - that would be a change to a schema frozen
                the day before, and the panel is one key press away.
            -->
            <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
            <Style.Triggers>
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Showing}" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                </DataTrigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>
    </UserControl.Style>

    <UserControl.Resources>
        <!--
            ONE FIELD: WHAT IT IS CALLED, THE MARK IT WEARS, AND WHAT THIS ENTRY SAYS IN IT.

            THREE COLUMNS, TWO OF THEM SHARED ACROSS EVERY LINE OF EVERY SECTION - Grid.IsSharedSizeScope
            at the top of this file - so twenty-six labels stand in one column and twenty-six values
            start at one edge. Auto rather than a number, so the widest label decides and nothing is
            cut.

            THE MIDDLE COLUMN IS A GUTTER FOR THE LIST'S OWN MARKS. The status line wears the dot the
            list draws beside "Running", the start line wears the ring beside "Automatic", and the
            two disagreement lines wear the triangle - the same three styles, out of Themes/Marks.xaml
            by way of Themes/Details.xaml, bound to the same shape codes. The other twenty-two lines
            wear nothing and the gutter keeps its width for them, so a value with a mark and a value
            without one begin at the same place. `docs/11` 3.1: a state is a shape and a colour and a
            word, and this panel used to have the word alone.

            WHAT THE LINE WEARS IS A CODE ON THE LINE, NOT A DECISION MADE HERE. Themes/Details.xaml
            reads Wears for the face, Missing for the colour of a state that is not an answer, and
            the shape codes for the marks. The one thing this template decides is GEOMETRY, and the
            one piece of it that changes is prose: the description goes under its label at the full
            width of the panel, because 1251 characters in a column 189 points wide is forty lines
            beside a single word.

            TWO ROWS, AND THE SECOND IS EMPTY ON EVERY LINE BUT ONE. The prose trigger moves the value
            into it - without the row declared, Grid.Row="1" would be quietly clamped to the last row
            that exists - which is the first - and the description would stay beside its label in
            three columns, exactly where it was, with nothing anywhere saying the trigger did nothing.
        -->
        <DataTemplate x:Key="DetailLineTemplate">
            <Grid Margin="{StaticResource MarginDetailLine}">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="DetailLabel" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="DetailMark" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                </Grid.RowDefinitions>

                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                           Text="{Binding Label}"
                           Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                           Style="{StaticResource DetailLabelText}" />

                <Ellipse Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource DetailStatusMark}" />
                <Ellipse Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource DetailStartMark}" />
                <Path Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource DetailAgainstMark}" />

                <TextBlock x:Name="Value"
                           Grid.Column="2"
                           Text="{Binding Shown}"
                           Style="{StaticResource DetailValueText}" />
            </Grid>

            <DataTemplate.Triggers>
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Wears}" Value="prose">
                    <Setter TargetName="Value" Property="Grid.Row" Value="1" />
                    <Setter TargetName="Value" Property="Grid.Column" Value="0" />
                    <Setter TargetName="Value" Property="Grid.ColumnSpan" Value="3" />
                </DataTrigger>
            </DataTemplate.Triggers>
        </DataTemplate>

        <!--
            ONE HEADED GROUP OF FIELDS, AS A BLOCK WITH A LINE DOWN ITS LEFT SIDE. The headings are
            the column picker's own groups, decided once in ViewModels/Columns.cs, so somebody who
            learned where a field lives while choosing columns finds it in the same place while
            reading about an entry.

            THE BLOCK IS THE PLAN SHEET'S BLOCK - PanelBlock in Themes/Plan.xaml, renamed for this
            second use - and the heading is NOT the sheet's heading, on purpose: Themes/Details.xaml
            says why a form with a grey label column needs its section name in weight where a sheet
            of white sentences needs it in grey.

            SECTIONS RATHER THAN TABS, and that is a deliberate departure from the word "tabbed" in
            section B of the specification. At four sections with nothing to edit, one scroll is
            read faster than tabs that hide three quarters of the answer. What will undo that is
            events, history and recovery arriving - content nobody reads in one pass.

            The note is the sentence under a section whose lines nobody has asked the machine for
            yet, and it is empty on every other section, so it takes no room when there is nothing
            to say.
        -->
        <DataTemplate x:Key="DetailSectionTemplate">
            <Border Style="{StaticResource PanelBlock}">
                <StackPanel>
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Heading}"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                               ToolTip="{Binding Heading}"
                               Style="{StaticResource DetailSectionText}" />

                    <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Lines}"
                                  ItemTemplate="{StaticResource DetailLineTemplate}" />

                    <!--
                        UNDER the lines, not under the heading - photographed both ways on
                        2026-09-16. Above them it read as a sentence about the section, and the
                        lines it is about are the ones saying "not read" below it.
                    -->
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Note}"
                               Style="{StaticResource DetailNoteText}" />
                </StackPanel>
            </Border>
        </DataTemplate>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!--
        A SURFACE OF ITS OWN, AND AN EDGE ON THE SIDE THAT FACES THE LIST. Until 2026-09-16 the panel
        stood on the window's #202020 beside a list on the same #202020 with nothing between them -
        "everything runs together", in the owner's words. What it stands on now is what the plan
        sheet stands on (SurfacePanel), without the sheet's corner and margin: `docs/11` 3.12, a
        sheet floats over the window to ask a question, a panel sits beside the list to show
        something, and the two must not look like one another.
    -->
    <Border Style="{StaticResource DetailPanel}">
        <Grid>
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                <RowDefinition Height="*" />
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <!--
                BOTH NAMES, AND NEITHER STANDS ALONE - `ADR-14`. The display name reads as the
                heading because it is the one somebody recognises, on up to two lines before it
                gives way with an ellipsis, and the internal name is under it in the fixed face
                because it is the one they will type into a command - the same face the plan sheet
                gives the manager's name, for the same reason. Each carries the whole of itself in
                a tooltip, exactly as every cell in the list does.
            -->
            <Grid Grid.Row="0">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <StackPanel Grid.Column="0">
                    <TextBlock AutomationProperties.AutomationId="detailsTitle"
                               Text="{Binding ShownLabel}"
                               ToolTip="{Binding ShownLabel}"
                               Style="{StaticResource DetailTitleText}" />

                    <TextBlock AutomationProperties.AutomationId="detailsName"
                               Text="{Binding ShownName}"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                               ToolTip="{Binding ShownName}"
                               Style="{StaticResource FixedText}" />
                </StackPanel>

                <!--
                    A VISIBLE WAY OUT, not only a key. Escape closes this and the tooltip says so, but
                    a panel whose only exit is a key nobody was told about is the third of Nielsen's
                    heuristics broken - `docs/11` 9.2 names it as this product's weakest. It is also
                    the only thing in this panel the keyboard stops on, which is why the panel itself
                    is not a tab stop.
                -->
                <Button Grid.Column="1"
                        AutomationProperties.AutomationId="detailsClose"
                        Content="{DynamicResource gui.details.close}"
                        ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.details.close.hint}"
                        Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                        VerticalAlignment="Top"
                        Click="CloseRequested" />
            </Grid>

            <!--
                THE ENTRY LEFT THE MACHINE WHILE THIS WAS OPEN, and rule 8 is why this line exists: a
                panel that carried on showing the last reading would answer questions about a service
                that is not there. It says the values below are the last thing read rather than blanking
                them, because what was read is still the most useful thing anybody has.
            -->
            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="detailsNotice"
                       Text="{Binding Notice}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}">
                <TextBlock.Style>
                    <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource NoticeText}">
                        <Style.Triggers>
                            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Notice}" Value="">
                                <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                            </DataTrigger>
                        </Style.Triggers>
                    </Style>
                </TextBlock.Style>
            </TextBlock>

            <!--
                Scrolls vertically and never sideways. A value too long for the width wraps, because
                this panel is the place a person comes to READ a description of 1251 characters - the
                cell it came from is the place that trims. Named, because the code-behind scrolls it
                to the top whenever the panel is opened on an entry - and only then.
            -->
            <ScrollViewer x:Name="Body"
                          AutomationProperties.AutomationId="detailsBody"
                          Grid.Row="2"
                          VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                          HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                <ItemsControl AutomationProperties.AutomationId="detailSections"
                              ItemsSource="{Binding Sections}"
                              ItemTemplate="{StaticResource DetailSectionTemplate}" />
            </ScrollViewer>
        </Grid>
    </Border>
</UserControl>
